        <!-- What to Look For -->
        <h2 id="what-to-look-for" class="scroll-mt-24">What to Look for in a Home Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          Not all treadmill specs matter equally, and marketing copy loves to bury the numbers that actually predict how a machine will feel three years into ownership. Here's what we actually weighted during testing.
        </p>

        <h3>Motor Horsepower</h3>
        <p>
          The single most important spec is <strong>continuous duty horsepower (CHP)</strong>, not the "peak horsepower" number some brands advertise, which measures a burst the motor can't sustain. Continuous horsepower is what the motor can output all day, every day, without overheating or wearing out prematurely. Budget machines typically run <strong>2.5-2.75 CHP</strong>, fine for walking, but likely to strain under sustained running. Mid-range machines land around <strong>3.0-3.25 CHP</strong>. Serious runners and multi-user households should look for <strong>3.5 CHP or higher</strong>, which is where premium home machines and the low end of commercial-grade equipment start.
        </p>

        <h3>Running Deck Size</h3>
        <p>
          Deck size is measured width by length, and it directly affects comfort and safety at speed. A <strong>20 x 55 inch</strong> deck is workable for walkers under six feet tall, but taller users or runners need more room. We consider <strong>20 x 60 inches</strong> the practical minimum for regular running, and <strong>22 x 60 inches</strong> or larger is noticeably more comfortable for anyone over six feet or with a longer stride.
        </p>

        <h3>Weight Capacity</h3>
        <p>
          Weight capacity isn't just about supporting a heavier user safely, it's a proxy for overall build quality. A treadmill rated for <strong>375 lbs</strong> generally has a sturdier frame, better bearings, and a more durable motor than one rated for <strong>250 lbs</strong>, even if the lighter-rated machine would technically support your body weight. As a rule, choose a capacity at least 50 pounds above the heaviest expected user.
        </p>

        <h3>Cushioning and Belt System</h3>
        <p>
          Cushioning systems range from simple foam padding to multi-zone shock absorption with individually tuned response points. Softer cushioning reduces joint impact, which matters most for runners and anyone managing knee or hip issues, while firmer decks feel more responsive and "ground-like" for speed work. Belt thickness and quality also affect long-term durability; thin belts on budget machines tend to need replacement sooner.
        </p>

        <h3>Incline Range</h3>
        <p>
          Incline training burns more calories per minute and builds strength differently than flat running. Entry-level machines often top out around <strong>0-10%</strong> incline with no decline option. Better machines offer <strong>0-15%</strong>, and premium models add <strong>decline capability down to -3%</strong>, letting you simulate downhill terrain, a feature that genuinely changes the character of a workout, not just a marketing checkbox.
        </p>

        <h3>Connectivity and App Ecosystem</h3>
        <p>
          Most current treadmills connect to some training ecosystem, whether it's <strong>iFit</strong>, the <strong>Peloton App</strong>, <strong>JRNY</strong>, <strong>Kinomap</strong>, or simple Bluetooth pairing with third-party fitness apps. These ecosystems vary a lot in cost and depth; some require ongoing subscriptions to unlock adaptive coaching and scenic content, while others are free add-ons layered on top of a fully functional standalone machine. Decide up front whether you want an interactive, subscription-driven experience or a simpler, self-directed one.
        </p>

        <h3>Noise Level</h3>
        <p>
          Motor and belt noise varies more between models than most buyers expect, and it matters enormously if your treadmill will share walls with a bedroom, a downstairs neighbor, or a home office. During testing, quieter machines were noticeably more pleasant for early-morning or late-night sessions, and a dedicated treadmill mat reduced perceived noise on every machine we tested.
        </p>

