        <!-- How to Choose -->
        <h2 id="how-to-choose" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">How to Choose the Right Treadmill for YOUR Home</h2>
        <p>
          The "best" treadmill depends entirely on who's using it and how. Here's how we'd narrow the field based on your specific situation.
        </p>

        <h3>By Body Type and Height</h3>
        <p>
          If you're under 5'8", most decks in this guide, including the Schwinn 810's smaller 20 x 55 inch surface, will feel comfortable. Taller users, especially anyone over 6 feet, should prioritize a <strong>60-inch or longer deck</strong>, which rules out the Schwinn and points toward the Sole F80, Bowflex Treadmill 22, or NordicTrack Commercial 1750, all of which offer more room for a full stride at running speeds.
        </p>

        <h3>By Primary Use: Walking vs. Running vs. HIIT</h3>
        <p>
          Walkers and light joggers can get excellent value from lower-horsepower machines like the <strong>Schwinn 810</strong> or <strong>Horizon 7.0 AT</strong>, both of which handle everyday walking workouts without issue and cost considerably less than running-focused machines. Regular runners should look toward <strong>3.5 CHP or higher</strong> motors, favoring the Sole F80, Horizon 7.8 AT, or NordicTrack Commercial 1750. If your workouts lean toward HIIT with frequent, sharp speed and incline changes, prioritize a treadmill with fast-responding incline motors and a stable frame, where the Horizon 7.8 AT and NordicTrack Commercial 1750 stood out in our testing.
        </p>

        <h3>By Space Constraints</h3>
        <p>
          Apartment dwellers and anyone working with a small room should look for machines with a <strong>folding deck and a compact footprint</strong>. The ProForm Pro 2000 and Horizon 7.0 AT both fold down efficiently and take up less floor space than the Bowflex Treadmill 22, which has the largest overall footprint in this guide and needs a genuinely dedicated area.
        </p>

        <h3>By Household: Multiple Users</h3>
        <p>
          If more than one person, especially people of different heights and weights, will regularly use the machine, prioritize a <strong>higher weight capacity</strong> and a more adjustable incline/cushioning system so each user can tailor the experience. The Sole F80's 375 lb capacity and adjustable cushioning make it a strong pick for multi-user households, as does the NordicTrack Commercial 1750's FlexSelect system.
        </p>

        <h3>By Budget Tier</h3>
        <p>
          Under $1,000, the <strong>Schwinn 810</strong> and <strong>Horizon 7.0 AT</strong> deliver the most reliable full-size experience. Between $1,000 and $1,500, the <strong>ProForm Pro 2000</strong> is our top pick for interactive training value. From $1,500 to $2,000, the <strong>Sole F80</strong> and <strong>Horizon 7.8 AT</strong> both deliver premium-feeling builds. Above $2,000, the <strong>NordicTrack Commercial 1750</strong> and <strong>Bowflex Treadmill 22</strong> represent the top of the home-use market covered in this guide.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-black text-amber-800 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">Practical Tip</p>
          <p class="text-amber-900 leading-relaxed">
            Before buying, measure your available space with roughly <strong>2 feet of clearance on all sides</strong> and the treadmill's folded and unfolded footprints in hand. It's the single most common mistake we hear about from readers after the fact, ordering a machine that technically fits the room but leaves no safe clearance for stepping off or walking around it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Setup and Maintenance -->
        <h2 id="setup-and-maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">Treadmill Setup, Break-In, and Maintenance</h2>

        <h3>Where to Place It</h3>
        <p>
          Set your treadmill on a hard, level surface, hardwood, tile, or a dedicated exercise mat over carpet, never directly on thick carpet, which can trap heat around the motor and cause the machine to sit unevenly. Leave at least <strong>two feet of clearance</strong> on all sides and behind the machine, and make sure you have roughly <strong>8 feet of ceiling height</strong> if you plan to use taller users' full range of motion at higher inclines. Good airflow around the motor housing also helps prevent premature overheating.
        </p>

        <h3>Assembly Time Expectations</h3>
        <p>
          Most home treadmills in this guide take <strong>60 to 120 minutes</strong> to assemble with two people, and we'd budget closer to the higher end for larger, feature-rich machines like the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 or Horizon 7.8 AT. Simpler machines like the Schwinn 810 typically assemble faster. Many retailers offer paid white-glove assembly if you'd rather skip the process entirely.
        </p>

        <h3>Belt Lubrication Schedule</h3>
        <p>
          Most belts need fresh silicone lubricant roughly every <strong>150-300 miles</strong>, though some newer decks come pre-lubricated with a longer interval; check your manual for the specific recommendation. Skipping lubrication accelerates belt and deck wear and is one of the fastest ways to shorten a treadmill's usable life.
        </p>

        <h3>Belt Alignment and Centering</h3>
        <p>
          Belts drift off-center over time, especially in the first few weeks of use as everything settles in. Most machines have two adjustment bolts at the rear roller; small quarter-turn adjustments, checked after a short test walk, are usually enough to recenter a drifting belt. Address it early, since a persistently off-center belt can wear unevenly and eventually need full replacement.
        </p>

        <h3>Cleaning Routine</h3>
        <p>
          Wipe down the deck, rails, and console after each use to prevent sweat buildup, and vacuum underneath and around the motor housing every few weeks to keep dust and pet hair from clogging ventilation. A quick weekly wipe-down takes less than five minutes and meaningfully extends the life of the belt and electronics.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
